## 🎯 Day 55 Objective

Days 53 and 54 went deep on one system. This one zooms out to the **program**: a maturity model a CISO, CDO, or AI governance lead can run as a two-hour workshop and walk out with a scored baseline, a defensible target state, and a sequenced roadmap.

Built to be **assessable, not aspirational** — every level descriptor is written as an observable condition, not an adjective. If two assessors can't independently land on the same score, the rubric has failed.

---

## 📉 The Problem This Model Solves

The 2026 data says the gap isn't funding or intent. It's operationalisation:

| Signal | Figure | Source |
|--------|--------|--------|
| Organisations with funding allocated to AI governance | **90%** | [Schellman, 525 US professionals](https://www.schellman.com/blog/news/new-schellman-ai-research-report) |
| Believe they'd pass an AI compliance audit today | **74%** | Schellman |
| Describe their governance program as fully mature | **27%** | Schellman |
| Executives claiming an AI governance framework exists | **87%** | Industry survey data |
| Have actually operationalised enterprise AI governance | **<25%** | Industry survey data |
| Adopted ISO/IEC 42001 | **36%** | Stanford HAI |
| Adopted NIST AI RMF | **33%** | Stanford HAI |
| Plan to adopt agentic AI within two years | **74%** | [Deloitte](https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/services/consulting/articles/iso-42001-standard-ai-governance-risk-management.html) |
| Have a mature governance model for AI **agents** | **21%** | Deloitte |
| Fortune 500 procurement teams planning to require ISO 42001 of vendors by 2027 | **83%** | Gartner, 2026 |

**Read the two most important rows together: 74% believe they'd pass an audit; 27% are actually mature.** That ~47-point delta is not dishonesty — it's the absence of a rubric. People assess themselves against their intentions. A maturity model's real job is to make the self-assessment falsifiable.

**And the agentic row is the one that should worry a board:** 74% adopting, 21% governed. Days 53–54 exist because that gap is where the next class of incidents comes from.

