## 📊 The Rubric

### D1 — Strategy, Policy & Accountability

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | No AI policy. AI decisions made per-team, per-project |
| 2 | AI policy published. No named owner; no enforcement mechanism; policy not referenced in any gate |
| 3 | Board-approved AI policy with responsible-AI principles. A **named individual** (not a committee) is accountable. Governance forum meets on a defined cadence with decision rights |
| 4 | Policy compliance is measured; exceptions require documented risk acceptance with expiry dates; governance KPIs reported to the board quarterly |
| 5 | Policy is versioned against regulatory and threat-landscape change; risk appetite is quantified and drives investment allocation |

*ISO 42001: Cl. 5.1–5.3, A.2, A.3 · NIST AI RMF: GOVERN 1.1–1.4, 2.1*

---

### D2 — AI Inventory & Risk Classification

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | No inventory. Shadow AI unknown and unmeasured |
| 2 | Partial inventory, manually maintained, stale within a quarter. Classification informal |
| 3 | Complete inventory of AI systems including embedded vendor AI. Documented risk-tiering methodology applied consistently. Each entry has an owner |
| 4 | Inventory is **discovery-driven**, not self-declared — populated by automated detection across cloud, SaaS, and endpoints. Reconciled continuously; drift alerts fire |
| 5 | Inventory extends to dynamic composition (agents, tools, MCP servers, models) via AgBOM; changes post-approval trigger automatic re-review |

*ISO 42001: A.4, A.5 · NIST AI RMF: MAP 1.1–1.6 · EU AI Act: Art. 6, Annex III classification*

> **Assessment tip:** ask for the inventory before the workshop. The gap between what arrives and what people describe in the room is your most honest signal for the whole assessment.

---

### D3 — Risk & Impact Assessment

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | No AI-specific risk assessment. Security review only, if any |
| 2 | Ad hoc assessment for high-profile projects. Format varies by reviewer |
| 3 | Mandatory AI impact assessment before deployment, covering bias, explainability, safety, privacy, and rights impacts. Standard template; sign-off required to ship |
| 4 | Assessments are risk-tiered (depth scales with tier), tracked to closure with SLAs, and reassessed on material change. Threat modelling required for agentic systems |
| 5 | Quantified risk (likelihood × impact, expressed in business terms); assessment outputs feed control selection automatically; post-deployment outcomes validate or correct the original assessment |

*ISO 42001: Cl. 6.1.2 (AI system impact assessment), Cl. 8.2 · NIST AI RMF: MAP 3, 5; MEASURE 2 · EU AI Act: Art. 9 (risk management), Art. 27 (FRIA)*

---

### D4 — Data Governance for AI

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | Training and retrieval data used without provenance, consent basis, or classification |
| 2 | Data classification exists for the enterprise but is not applied to AI pipelines specifically |
| 3 | Documented lineage for training and RAG data: source, licence, consent basis, classification, retention. Bias and quality checks performed and recorded |
| 4 | Automated enforcement — pipelines reject unclassified or out-of-policy data; bias metrics tracked per release; drift monitored against a baseline |
| 5 | **Provenance is a first-class runtime attribute**: every span entering a model context carries a trust tag, enabling enforcement (see Day 54, ASI01/ASI06) |

*ISO 42001: A.7 (data for AI systems) · NIST AI RMF: MAP 2.3, MEASURE 2.8 · EU AI Act: Art. 10*

> L5 here is the quiet dependency for everything in Day 54. A control plane can only act on provenance if the data layer produces it. Most organisations discover their agent security project is actually a data governance project — and the earlier that lands, the cheaper it is.

---

### D5 — Lifecycle Assurance & Testing

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | Ship when it demos well. No pre-release AI-specific testing |
| 2 | Functional evals only. No adversarial testing. No documented acceptance criteria |
| 3 | Defined gates across the lifecycle with pass/fail criteria: evals, safety testing, red-team exercise for high-risk systems, documented model cards |
| 4 | Automated eval suites in CI; regression thresholds block release; red teaming is scheduled and scoped by risk tier; findings tracked with owners and due dates |
| 5 | Continuous adversarial testing in production-like conditions; replay/digital-twin validation before any policy or capability expansion; assurance evidence generated as a build artifact |

*ISO 42001: A.6.2.2–A.6.2.5, Cl. 9.1 · NIST AI RMF: MEASURE 1–4 · EU AI Act: Art. 15 (accuracy, robustness), Art. 17 (QMS)*

---

### D6 — Runtime Controls & Monitoring

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | No runtime controls. Prompt instructions treated as controls |
| 2 | Basic input/output filtering. No logging sufficient for reconstruction |
| 3 | Enforced guardrails at defined control points; human oversight required for high-impact actions; logs capture decision, input, output, identity |
| 4 | **Declarative, version-controlled policy enforced at runtime** with allow/deny/modify verdicts; behavioural baselines with drift alerting; blast-radius caps and tested kill switches |
| 5 | Policy is portable across platforms (ACS-style hook architecture); enforcement telemetry feeds detection engineering; intent-deviation monitoring live |

*ISO 42001: A.6.2.6, A.9, Cl. 9.1 · NIST AI RMF: MANAGE 2.2, 4.1 · EU AI Act: Art. 14 (human oversight), Art. 12 (logging)*

> This is the domain [Day 54](../Day54) implements end to end. **L4 in D6 is not reachable without ACS-style hooks or an equivalent** — manual oversight cannot gate an agent acting in milliseconds. This is the single clearest instance in the model where maturity has a hard architectural prerequisite.

---

### D7 — Third-Party & Supply Chain

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | Vendor AI adopted with no AI-specific diligence |
| 2 | Standard vendor security questionnaire only; no AI-specific questions |
| 3 | AI-specific vendor assessment: model provenance, training data claims, subprocessors, retention, evaluation evidence. Contract terms cover AI use |
| 4 | AIBOM maintained for material dependencies; components pinned and signature-verified; continuous vendor monitoring; ISO 42001 alignment required in procurement |
| 5 | Supply chain transparency is dynamic — runtime discovery of new tools/MCP servers/models triggers governance review before use |

*ISO 42001: A.10 (third parties and suppliers) · NIST AI RMF: MAP 4.1, GOVERN 6.1 · EU AI Act: Art. 25 (value chain) · ISO 27001: A.5.19–5.21*

> **83% of Fortune 500 procurement teams plan to require ISO 42001 alignment from vendors by 2027.** For anyone selling into the enterprise, D7 maturity flips from a cost centre to a revenue prerequisite inside eighteen months.

---

### D8 — Incident Response, Assurance & Continuous Improvement

| L | Observable condition |
|---|----------------------|
| 1 | No AI incident definition. AI failures handled as ordinary bugs |
| 2 | AI incidents routed to the general IR process, which has no AI-specific playbook or severity criteria |
| 3 | AI incident taxonomy and runbooks exist (bias incident, model failure, injection, data leakage); severity criteria defined; regulatory notification paths mapped |
| 4 | Runbooks tested via tabletop; MTTD/MTTR measured for AI incidents; post-incident findings drive control changes with tracked closure; internal audit covers AI controls |
| 5 | Agent-specific scenarios covered (rogue agent, memory poisoning, cascading failure) with automated containment; external assurance obtained; lessons feed the risk model and re-tier systems automatically |

*ISO 42001: Cl. 9.2–9.3, Cl. 10 · NIST AI RMF: MANAGE 4.1–4.3 · EU AI Act: Art. 73 (serious incident reporting), Art. 72 (post-market monitoring)*

